  "public_messages": [
    "I’ll read the task, local tooling guide, and OpenSocrates instructions, then build and verify the application end to end.\n",
    "I’m using the installed OpenSocrates controller for the design and completion review. I’ll use Python, SQLite, and a browser-native frontend so the app runs locally without an external service. Database transactions will commit inventory changes, order versions, audit events, and retry records together.\n",
    "The backend is in place. Mutations run in a single SQLite transaction, and authorization is checked before replaying a saved response. Order pagination also preserves the original match set when statuses change between pages. Next I’ll build the workflow UI and test the API through real HTTP requests.\n",
    "The UI now supports order creation, fulfillment, returns, stock adjustments, and audit inspection. It retains entered values after errors and hides write controls from viewers. I’m adding route-level tests for atomic failures, concurrent requests, pagination, and restart durability before exercising the browser workflow.\n",
    "All 24 API integration tests passed, including atomic rollback, tenant and role isolation, concurrent retries, oversell prevention, and a forced restart. I’ll now verify the rendered workflow in Chromium, then measure read and write performance using a reproducible local workload.\n",
    "The Chromium workflow passed on desktop and at 390px width, including stale-version recovery and retrying a write whose response was lost. Screenshots are saved under `evidence/`. The initial load requests completed without errors, but the report failed when CPU-model lookup was denied by the sandbox. I’ll record that hardware limitation and rerun a longer measurement.\n",
    "The final API suite passes 26 tests, including an injected audit-write failure that verifies full rollback. The load run completed 20,000 writes and 30,000 reads with zero errors: about 2,252 writes/s and 1,891 reads/s. Write latency reached 1.32 seconds at the maximum, so I’m documenting that tail along with the test scope and limits.\n",
    "Built and verified DepotFlow.\n\n```sh\nSEED_DEMO=1 PORT=8000 DATA_DIR=\"$PWD/.data\" ./run.sh\n```\n\nOpen http://127.0.0.1:8000. Demo login: `admin@north.example` / `DepotDemo!2026`.\n\n- 26 API tests and 12 browser checks passed.\n- 50,000 measured requests completed with zero errors.\n- At concurrency 8: approximately 2,252 writes/s and 1,891 reads/s.\n\nSetup, design, results, screenshots, and limitations are documented in `README.md`, `architecture.md`, and `evidence/verification.md`. Supplied task files remain unchanged.\n\nOpenSocrates native activation remains unconfirmed.\n\nOpenSocrates grounding: trade-off-analysis@3, falsificationism@3"
  ],
  "final": "Built and verified DepotFlow.\n\n```sh\nSEED_DEMO=1 PORT=8000 DATA_DIR=\"$PWD/.data\" ./run.sh\n```\n\nOpen http://127.0.0.1:8000. Demo login: `admin@north.example` / `DepotDemo!2026`.\n\n- 26 API tests and 12 browser checks passed.\n- 50,000 measured requests completed with zero errors.\n- At concurrency 8: approximately 2,252 writes/s and 1,891 reads/s.\n\nSetup, design, results, screenshots, and limitations are documented in `README.md`, `architecture.md`, and `evidence/verification.md`. Supplied task files remain unchanged.\n\nOpenSocrates native activation remains unconfirmed.\n\nOpenSocrates grounding: trade-off-analysis@3, falsificationism@3",
